      "despre.eyebrow":"Who we are","despre.h2":"About our church",
      "despre.teaser":"Grace Romanian Baptist Church of Sacramento is a Romanian-speaking Baptist church community, serving families in the Sacramento area since 1990.",
      "despre.readmore":"About us and our church history",
      "despre.p1":"Grace Romanian Baptist Church of Sacramento was founded on December 25, 1990, out of a desire to give the Romanian community a place of worship, biblical teaching, and genuine friendship. We are members of the Romanian Baptist Association in USA and Canada, as well as the Northern Baptist Church Association.",
      "despre.p2":"We believe in the power of God's Word, in prayer, and in the fellowship of believers, following the pattern set out in Acts 2:42-47. Every family finds a welcoming place here.",
      "history.eyebrow":"Where we started","history.h2":"Our History",
      "history.text":"\"If it had not been the Lord who was on our side, let now Israel say. Our help is in the name of the Lord who made heaven and earth.\" Psalm 124:1,8. Grace Romanian Baptist Church (GRBC) was founded on December 25th, 1990, at the initiative of a group of Romanian Baptists who wanted to worship God and faithfully serve Him.\n\nUnder the guidance of a Godly leader of the Romanian community in the US diaspora, pastor Pitt Popovici, and directed by God's grace, this group of Christians formed a church open to anyone who truthfully and faithfully wanted to worship God. At first, the church met in an annex of a Nazarene church on Arden Way. In February 1991, the church invited Pastor Nelu Cintean, who served as pastor for six months before moving to San Leandro for a new ministry opportunity; he continued to be a member of GRBC and helped the local ministry whenever necessary, collaborating with Pastor Pitt Popovici, who was serving as senior pastor at the time. God blessed the church under Pastor Pitt's ministry, and it grew through the addition of families from the Sacramento area, as well as some who moved from Los Angeles and the Bay Area. Soon after, a new building was bought in West Sacramento. Pastor Pitt continued to serve until the summer of 1996, when he moved to Atlanta, GA. After his departure, Pastor Tiberiu Rata took over while still attending Golden Gate Seminary; during this time the church also bought a new sanctuary at its current address, 3832 Whitney Ave, Sacramento, CA 95821. Under Pastor Tibi's guidance, the youth ministry expanded considerably and a wonderful youth choir was formed. Pastor Tibi served for two years, moving to Alabama in 1998 to continue his theological studies, and Pastor Nelu Cintean once again stepped in during the transitional period. In January 1999, Pastor Mircea Filip arrived from Arizona to serve at GRBC; during his one year of service, it became clear the sanctuary — by then over fifty years old — was in great need of major repairs. Due to personal circumstances, Pastor Filip returned to Arizona in January 2000.\n\nPastor Claudiu Valcu began serving in June 2000, a young graduate of the Baptist Theological Institute in Bucharest, Romania, who had immigrated to the US with his wife Sanda just two months prior. Construction on the new sanctuary began in August 2000, and on January 20th, 2001, Pastor Claudiu was ordained as pastor of Grace Romanian Baptist Church, continuing as senior pastor ever since in close collaboration with Pastor Nelu Cintean, who served as assistant pastor. In September 2008, Pastor Stefan Ghinescu joined the pastoral team, serving as youth pastor until November 2011 while also pursuing a Master's degree at Western Seminary. Though 2001 was a difficult year, as several families left to form another church, God blessed GRBC, and in August 2001 the new 300-seat sanctuary was dedicated — we remain grateful for the great sacrifice of the many members who contributed to that project. Some of the families who left in 2001 later returned.\n\nThe Lord blessed our church abundantly, and over 12 years it grew from 74 to over 186 active members, and over 250 counting children and youth. Between 2005 and 2007, a state-of-the-art educational building was added, including a 180-seat multi-purpose room, four Sunday school rooms, a commercial-grade kitchen, and new restrooms — made possible by the dedicated, consistent sacrifice of many families in our church.\n\nToday, GRBC enjoys a season of peace and growth, serving its members and the broader Romanian community in the Sacramento area, in collaboration with other Romanian churches in the area and with the churches of the Romanian Baptist Association in the US and Canada, as well as the Northern Baptist Association. Looking back at the way God has blessed His people over the years, we can only thank and praise Him, declaring EBEN-EZER: \"Thus far the Lord has helped us!\" 1 Samuel 7:12.",
